WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:06.800
SCP-173. It’s a peculiar statue with a bad&nbsp;
attitude and a penchant for strangulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.400
and snapping necks, though only if nobody&nbsp;
has maintained unbroken eye contact with it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:17.520
Some know it as The Sculpture, some know it as&nbsp;
The Peanut, some know it as probably the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.640
important anomaly of all, considering it’s the&nbsp;
one that got this whole crazy affair started.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:28.000
But today we’re here to ask an important&nbsp;
question: Do any of us really know it at all?

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.320
After all, its original file is hardly&nbsp;
comprehensive. You might even venture to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:38.400
say it’s one of the most enigmatic SCPs out there,&nbsp;
despite its popularity, but today, we’re taking a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:44.320
dive beneath the concrete, rebar, and Krylon-brand&nbsp;
spray paint to give you the real inside scoop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:51.360
on SCP-173. We’ve broken into the SCP Foundation&nbsp;
Archive and found ten different important files on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:57.840
this infamous vertebrae vandal, and we’re going to&nbsp;
explore each and every one of them with you today.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:03.040
As you can expect from the weird and wild&nbsp;
SCP Foundation, they cover possible pasts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:08.160
presents, and futures, and some of them will&nbsp;
naturally contradict. But that just gives you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.200
more of an opportunity to immerse yourself and&nbsp;
compare them. We often like to believe that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.560
real truth is somewhere in between, so make sure&nbsp;
you pay attention to every detail throughout.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:24.800
So let’s get this show on the road, and explore&nbsp;
our ten documents of interest for SCP-173&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:30.320
in hopes of finally getting the complete&nbsp;
story. And of course...Don’t blink.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.160
Document One: Crunch.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:35.760
Where better to start than the mind&nbsp;
of the sinister sculpture itself?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:40.880
“Crunch” gives us an insight into the private&nbsp;
thoughts of this otherwise pretty uncommunicative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:45.200
monster and reveals a great deal about its&nbsp;
relationship with its favorite activity:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.480
Snapping the necks of anyone&nbsp;
who dares to look away from it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:55.520
In Crunch, SCP-173 speaks in very basic, broken,&nbsp;
Rorschach-esque English, saying things like:

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:02.640
“Room dark. People gone. Can move. I happy.

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:13.040
Trapped for years. Kept by men for "research".&nbsp;
Left alone in room, left to scratch at walls.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:22.720
Left to rot and die unless needed. Specimen for&nbsp;
"research". Trapped by self. Never seen by others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:30.080
never spoken. Listen, but never&nbsp;
speak. See, but never seen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:39.280
Made of rock when others come. Free when they&nbsp;
not look. Prisoner when they look back. I hate.”

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:46.400
SCP-173 hates and resents its captors&nbsp;
for keeping it confined. In its own mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:51.520
“Crunch” - how it refers to the snapping of its&nbsp;
victims’ neck - is the only thing that gives its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:56.480
life a sense of meaning. It will continue&nbsp;
to Crunch for as long as it’s contained,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:02.000
because thanks to the SCP Foundation,&nbsp;
Crunch is all it has left these days.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.600
Document Two: How 173 Got to Site-19.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:10.160
This semi-origin story is one that fans&nbsp;
of this channel will be familiar with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:15.520
While it doesn’t give us an insight into why&nbsp;
this nasty pile of concrete actually exists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:18.960
it does inform us why it’s currently our problem.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:23.920
SCP-173 isn’t actually an anomaly&nbsp;
native to our universe. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:29.120
its native dimension was destroyed&nbsp;
by the beast trapped in SCP-2317:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:35.200
The Devourer of Worlds. As is standard procedure&nbsp;
for a universe about to experience an XK-Class End&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:39.280
of the World Scenario, the Foundation’s&nbsp;
Department of Extra-Dimensional Affairs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:43.920
swooped in and collected all the valuable&nbsp;
anomalies before the world went down the tube.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:51.760
One of these was, of course, SCP-173, which was&nbsp;
vacated to Site-19 in our dimension in 1993.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.880
Do you think we would have been better&nbsp;
off if we just left him for the devourer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:59.200
of worlds to take care of? Let us know what&nbsp;
you think about that down in the comments.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.520
Document Three: The Black Moon.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:07.520
Sometimes, you can be your own worst enemy.&nbsp;
If the file on SCP-001, The Black Moon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:14.027
is to be believed, the SCP Foundation may actually&nbsp;
be behind the creation of this ornery objet d'art&nbsp;

00:04:14.027 --> 00:04:19.200
- though in the Foundation’s defense, it was in&nbsp;
service of potentially fighting off a far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:25.280
deadly foe, the ominous extra-dimensional&nbsp;
being known only as the Black Moon.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:28.160
One attempt to stop the Black&nbsp;
Moon from destroying the universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:33.200
was known as the KEY Project, which involved&nbsp;
creating a number of items linked to the Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:37.600
Moon via conceptual engineering that&nbsp;
would hopefully nullify its effects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:42.560
The objects needed the following qualities:&nbsp;
An inability to act while under observation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:48.480
a hostility towards human life, and the capacity&nbsp;
to end conscious life if given the opportunity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:55.520
Those three descriptors fit SCP-173 to a tee, and&nbsp;
it was considered the project’s greatest success.

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:00.400
However, it sadly wasn’t enough to actually&nbsp;
stop the horrific rampage of the Black Moon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:05.200
The researcher managing the case, Dr. Moto,&nbsp;
was so ashamed by the failure that he willingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:09.840
entered SCP-173’s containment chamber,&nbsp;
and got his neck snapped in the process.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:16.000
Document Four: Conclusions&nbsp;
RE: D-173-2263 Incident.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.520
This document finds its origins in&nbsp;
the video analysis of a Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:25.280
researcher with an incredibly powerful&nbsp;
name: Dr. Delano Smascher. Dr. Smascher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:30.800
had closely observed video footage of SCP-173&nbsp;
killing several members of D-Class personnel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:35.840
and began formulating a new theory about&nbsp;
how 173 actually killed its victims.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:41.440
Dr. Smascher theorized, after all, that&nbsp;
something with arms as short as SCP-173&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:46.800
and no opposable digits would have trouble&nbsp;
effectively strangling or snapping human necks.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:52.080
According to Dr. Smascher’s observations,&nbsp;
SCP-173 doesn’t actually make any physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:57.040
contact with its victims. Instead, the victims&nbsp;
feel oddly compelled to look at the creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:01.920
if ever nothing is looking at it. Their eyes&nbsp;
will snap open, and their heads will twist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:06.720
at any angle they need to in order to look&nbsp;
at the creature in the eyes. It’s the sheer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:12.640
force of this sudden change that often leads to&nbsp;
snapped necks and bruised faces for the victims,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:18.320
not the Sculpture itself physically causing&nbsp;
the damage. If Dr. Smascher is to be believed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:24.720
the Sculpture is deadly purely because it simply&nbsp;
demands to be looked at by somebody at all times.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:27.120
Document Five: Anniversary.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:30.640
This brings us nicely to our&nbsp;
next document: Anniversary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:35.280
positing a different theory for&nbsp;
the mysterious creation of SCP-173.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:40.400
This comes from the perspective of said possible&nbsp;
creator, an anomalous entity that we’ll call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:45.120
The Artist. Much like the unfortunate&nbsp;
Dr. Moto, The Artist, an intelligent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:51.600
being that can seemingly become invisible and&nbsp;
intangible at will, considered SCP-173 to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:58.240
its finest creation. It viewed SCP-173 as a&nbsp;
perfect representation of humanity: It does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:04.000
nothing but bleed and defecate, and if anyone&nbsp;
refuses to pay attention to it, it kills them.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:10.720
It was a perfect symbol of The Artist’s contempt&nbsp;
for humanity. It even easily snuck into Site-19 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:17.120
visit SCP-173 on the anniversary of its creation&nbsp;
and found the Foundation’s lack of appreciation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:22.720
for this fine creation. As an act of revenge for&nbsp;
keeping his creation locked up and out of sight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:29.440
for so long, he gave the sculpture permission,&nbsp;
just once, to kill someone who looked away, even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:36.880
if others were still looking at it. For SCP-173,&nbsp;
this would be considered a wonderful little treat.

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:39.680
Document Six: Her Masterpiece.

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:42.720
This next document, known as “Her Masterpiece”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:48.160
suggests that SCP-173 was created by a very&nbsp;
different kind of artist. While this potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:54.800
creator of SCP-173 was somehow able to produce&nbsp;
an anomaly, she was not anomalous herself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.800 --> 00:08:00.240
just motivated by rage and revenge. She’d been&nbsp;
pregnant in the past but lost the baby due to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:06.320
actions of a man whom she considered her greatest&nbsp;
enemy. She vowed to make art from his demise.

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:11.440
The woman murdered her enemy with a baseball&nbsp;
bat, before using concrete and rebar to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:17.840
SCP-173 around him, almost as a way to mock&nbsp;
her fallen foe. However, like something out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:24.240
of a horror movie, this act of murderous&nbsp;
creation somehow imbued SCP-173 with life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:28.720
When this spiteful artist had eventually&nbsp;
finished the creation and started to walk away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:34.000
it claimed her as its first victim. The last&nbsp;
thing she remembered hearing was the sound of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:40.320
concrete scraping against the ground, and then&nbsp;
a nasty crunch, before everything went dark...

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:43.040
Document Seven: RE: The Statue.

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:48.480
From one tragedy to another. The document&nbsp;
“RE: The Statue” shows the journey of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.600
Researcher Marcus Silburn from idealistic&nbsp;
young employee to full-on mental breakdown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:59.840
after trying and failing to gain control&nbsp;
of SCP-173. He entered the SCP-173 project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:04.320
with bright eyes and big ideas about how to&nbsp;
contain the Sculpture, suggesting that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:09.280
human element be removed entirely in order&nbsp;
to reduce risk to Foundation personnel.

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:13.600
He proposed ideas like introducing a&nbsp;
sprinkler and drainage system to the chamber&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:19.200
and using a sentient AI and remote viewing&nbsp;
technology to keep SCP-173 immobilized.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:25.280
However, he was then enlightened to some&nbsp;
frustrating and frightening facts about SCP-173:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:29.760
In the absence of actual present human&nbsp;
observation, the same build-up of feces and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:35.520
blood that the creature somehow excretes builds up&nbsp;
in any technology used to control or contain it.

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:40.160
It destroys the circuit boards of AIs,&nbsp;
takes flying camera drones out of the sky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:44.880
and even degrades the locks on its chamber&nbsp;
doors, leading to the brutal death of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:48.960
janitor in the hall. Researcher Silburn&nbsp;
advanced far enough through the ranks of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:54.960
the Foundation to be in direct communication&nbsp;
with Dr. Jack Bright, the Director of Site-19.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:59.840
After his final attempt at containing the creature&nbsp;
without direct human observation resulted in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:05.600
a containment breach and the death of twelve&nbsp;
people, Silburn was moved off the SCP-173 case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:11.360
after breaking down and begging for permission&nbsp;
to terminate the anomaly. Permission was denied.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:15.280
Dr. Bright closed the document with a note&nbsp;
to a member of the O5 Council reading,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:18.640
“It can be surmised that our&nbsp;
conclusion was correct- the object,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:24.080
for whatever reason, desires or requires&nbsp;
human attention, and will not be denied it.”

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:26.480
Document Eight: A Chance At Freedom.

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:30.640
We once again return to the&nbsp;
direct perspective of SCP-173,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:36.720
but this differs vastly from the perspective of&nbsp;
SCP-173’s inner monologue presented in Crunch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:39.760
According to the document&nbsp;
entitled A Chance At Freedom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:46.320
SCP-173 was actually an incredibly intelligent&nbsp;
agent of espionage sent from another dimension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:52.640
along with its unknown cadet, though the&nbsp;
cadet is heavily implied to be SCP-096.

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:56.800
This piece details a containment&nbsp;
breach from SCP-173’s perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:02.240
as it is shown looking down on humanity and its&nbsp;
primitive technology. It laments the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:07.440
it lost its weapon during the interdimensional&nbsp;
transfer, and seethes that if it still had its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:12.160
weapon, this all would have played out very&nbsp;
differently. Despite conceding that the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:17.520
it underestimated the humans was the main reason&nbsp;
it ended up in containment, it spends the majority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:24.000
of the document touting its own superiority,&nbsp;
until one of SCP-131 - The Eye Bots - turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:31.120
up and froze it in place. The degree to which&nbsp;
SCP-173 hates the Eye Bots cannot be overstated.

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:35.120
Document Nine: Experiences with SCP-682.

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:42.240
Of course, no “full story” video on SCP-173 would&nbsp;
be complete without a discussion of the SCP-682&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:47.440
cross tests. A number of researchers, though&nbsp;
particularly the very persistent Dr. Carlson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:54.240
decided to use SCP-173 in various manners in hopes&nbsp;
of finally doing in that damn lizard. In their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.240 --> 00:12:01.120
first engagement, 682 seemed rightfully frightened&nbsp;
of 173, and when a sniper shot its eyes out, 173&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:06.960
immediately snapped its neck. Of course, it grew&nbsp;
back a series of sniper-proof eyes not long after.

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:12.800
Someone next suggested SCP-173 being sent in&nbsp;
with a picture of SCP-096 taped to its chest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:17.120
though this was rejected as being a ridiculous&nbsp;
and incredibly dangerous idea to everyone.

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:24.640
The researchers then went after SCP-682 with&nbsp;
high precision blades, cutting it into pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:30.640
each of which became autonomously dangerous&nbsp;
entities. SCP-173 then began to murder each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:35.760
of these blobs of mutated tissue, until it was&nbsp;
stopped when a D-Class was sent in to stare at it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:42.080
while the rest of 682 regenerated. SCP-173 was&nbsp;
transported back to its cell, but there was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:47.600
brief power outage during transit, leading SCP-173&nbsp;
to kill a number of the staff transporting it.

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:50.880
This test was still considered&nbsp;
a step in the right direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:54.480
After SCP-682 was once again&nbsp;
shrunk by a different anomaly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.480 --> 00:13:01.520
SCP-173 was released into its chamber. At first,&nbsp;
it appeared that 173 had actually killed SCP-682,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:06.320
but a week later it returned, having reconstituted&nbsp;
itself from matter that had been splattered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:12.480
on SCP-173 after it’d been brutalized.&nbsp;
Making this test, once again, a failure.

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:17.600
Dr. Carlson requested the resources to conduct&nbsp;
one more cross-test, but this request was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:23.200
denied. A member of the O5 Council affixed a&nbsp;
request, simply saying, “Stop it, Carlson.”

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:31.200
Document Ten: Revised Entry.

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:38.960
Oh...that time SCP-173 literally caused an&nbsp;
XK-Class End of the World Scenario. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:43.760
document Revised Entry, we get a frightening&nbsp;
glimpse into a possible future world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:46.880
where SCP-173 develops a terrifying new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:52.000
anomalous ability: Multiplication. We&nbsp;
don’t know exactly how it happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:57.120
but here, the Sculpture somehow developed&nbsp;
the ability to make exact copies of itself.

00:13:57.120 --> 00:14:02.640
And what’s more, every time one of these&nbsp;
copies is produced, the collective SCP-173&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:08.480
hive mind gets stronger. Allowing them to think&nbsp;
tactically, and slowly wipe out North America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:11.760
even destroying any potential&nbsp;
Foundation countermeasures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:16.400
then ripping and snapping through&nbsp;
anyone or anything that dared oppose.

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:21.280
In the end, the world decided to take the&nbsp;
nuclear option - literally - and blow away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:26.720
North America with a nuclear bombardment in&nbsp;
order to stop the SCP-173 army from spreading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:32.160
to the rest of the world and killing everyone.&nbsp;
However, this attempt didn’t actually work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:39.120
as SCP-173s were spotted in Wales, over in the&nbsp;
UK, leading to a second nuclear bombardment.

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:44.560
It continued like this until there were no human&nbsp;
beings left to push the big red button anymore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.560 --> 00:14:48.720
and only the endless legions of SCP-173 remained.

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:52.800
There is one bright side to this&nbsp;
otherwise pretty bleak situation, though:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:56.640
A group of around 150 SCP-173s ganged up on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.640 --> 00:15:03.040
SCP-682 and tore it to shreds. Somewhere&nbsp;
out there, Dr. Carlson is probably smiling.

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:08.880
Now go check out “SCP-173 Origin&nbsp;
Story - How 173 Got to Site-19” and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:15.840
“The Eye Pods - SCP-131” for more tales involving&nbsp;
the SCP Foundation’s most psychopathic statue!

